{'question_id': 'qst_0022', 'context_precision': 0.9999999999, 'context_recall': 1.0, 'question': 'What is the recommended liability cap language in the procurement legal triage SOP for vendor contracts (including the key exceptions and how the cap amount is determined)?', 'response': 'The recommended limitation of liability language is: "Except for willful misconduct or breach of confidentiality, liability shall not exceed the greater of (i) USD 1,000,000 or (ii) the fees paid in the 12 months preceding the claim."\n\nThe key exceptions are willful misconduct and breach of confidentiality, and the cap amount is determined as the greater of USD 1,000,000 or the fees paid in the 12 months preceding the claim.', 'gold_answer': 'The SOP\'s recommended limitation of liability language is: "Except for willful misconduct or breach of confidentiality, liability shall not exceed the greater of (i) USD 1,000,000 or (ii) the fees paid in the 12 months preceding the claim." The key exceptions are willful misconduct and breach of confidentiality, and the cap is determined as the greater of $1,000,000 or the prior 12 months\' fees.', 'reference': 'The procurement legal triage SOP recommends limitation of liability language stating that liability shall not exceed the greater of (i) USD 1,000,000 or (ii) the fees paid in the 12 months preceding the claim.\nThe key exceptions to the SOPs liability cap are willful misconduct and breach of confidentiality.\nThe cap amount is determined as the greater of USD 1,000,000 or the fees paid in the 12 months prior to the claim.', 'retrieved_contexts': ['(e.g., critical hardware replacement) may be approved by Head of Procurement + Head of Finance with retroactive legal review. Document the emergency rationale and complete a post-facto risk assessment within 5 business days.For multi-year commitments with budget implications, Procurement must coordinate with Budget Owners and FP&A to surface commitment in the next forecast cycle.Template Clauses (common redlines and recommended language)Limitation of Liability (recommended cap): "Except for willful misconduct or breach of confidentiality, liability shall not exceed the greater of (i) USD 1,000,000 or (ii) the fees paid in the 12 months preceding the claim."Data Processing Addendum (DPA) pointer: "Attach DPA described in Appendix A; vendor must support EU SCCs and provide KMS integration details for customer-managed keys where required."Escrow/KMS: "For on-prem or model-hosting arrangements where customer datasets or model weights may be subject to e-discovery or regulatory requests,', "Procurement: Legal Risk Triage & Escalation SOPOverviewThis Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) describes Redwood Inference's end-to-end triage for procurement items that require legal review or cross-functional escalation. The goal is to reduce cycle time, ensure appropriate risk controls for contracting and billing, and provide clear ownership and SLA targets for each review tier.ScopeApplies to all vendor engagements, SOWs, license purchases, hardware leases (including GPU reservations), SaaS subscriptions, and professional services agreements initiated via Procurement.Excludes low-value purchases that follow the corporate purchasing card (P-card) policy (see Finance policy: /confluence/finance-and-legal/procurement/p-card-guidelines).DefinitionsLow-risk procurement: Purchase value <$25k, no data access, vendor uses standard market terms, no cross-border data transfer.Medium-risk procurement: $25k–$250k, includes limited data access (non-sensitive), or requires limited bespoke terms", '"For on-prem or model-hosting arrangements where customer datasets or model weights may be subject to e-discovery or regulatory requests, vendor must provide an escrow arrangement or KMS integration documented in Schedule 3."Audit & Reporting RequirementsQuarterly procurement compliance report to Finance and Legal: list of executed contracts > $100k and any deviations from SOP.Monthly aging report of contracts in negotiation (by risk band).Post-mortem on all high-risk negotiations that exceeded 30 business days: root cause and action items.Appendix A: Sample Legal Redline Checklist (for counsel)Verify IP ownership and license grants.Check indemnity and carve-outs for open-source dependencies.Confirm limitation of liability and insurance obligations.Ensure confidentiality duration and return/destruction procedures.Confirm termination for convenience and transition assistance obligations.Appendix B: Example CLI snippet to tag a contract in ContractDB (internal tool)$ contractdb tag add', 'Procedure (step-by-step)Procurement Request Intakea. Requestor opens a PR in the Procurement system (Jira/Workday) with required attachments: vendor quote, SOW, standard T&C, and vendor risk questionnaire.b. Procurement Owner tags the request with one of the risk bands and assigns Contracting Owner.Automated Pre-checks (Procurement)a. Validate vendor is in the approved vendor list and has completed KYC.b. Run vendor risk scan (third-party risk tool) — attach report.Legal Triage (Commercial Counsel)a. Counsel performs 3-screen review: (1) identify material exposures (indemnity, liability caps, warranty, IP, data access); (2) verify billing & rev-rec implications (see RevRec checklist below); (3) recommend standard redlines.b. Counsel applies template responses from the Clause Library for common items (data transfer addendum, limited indemnity, software license grant).Cross-functional Review (when required)a. Security: Required for any network connectivity, data access, KMS/escrow, or'], 'sources': ['dsid_f2f019c122154edc8b21f56879661043__procurement-legal-risk-escalation-sop-2026.txt']}
{'question_id': 'qst_0104', 'context_precision': 0.9999999999, 'context_recall': 1.0, 'question': 'What is the standard amount of time a new hire buddy is expected to spend per day during the first two weeks when a long-term contractor is converted to a full-time employee?', 'response': '1 hour/day for the first 2 weeks.', 'gold_answer': 'The buddy is expected to spend about 1 hour per day with the new hire during the first two weeks.', 'reference': 'During the first two weeks, the new hire buddy is expected to spend about 1 hour per day with the new hire.', 'retrieved_contexts': ['"Deliver a prototype and usability notes; present to cross-functional partners for feedback."Access and credentialing patterns (common scenarios):If contractor had vendor-managed credentials, issue new corporate credentials to avoid shared secrets.Rotate any keys or tokens used during contractor period on day 0 of FTE status.For sensitive systems, escalate privileges in two stages: limited access on day 1, expanded after 30-day review.Buddy program guidance (Peer Sponsor):Buddy time allocation: 1 hour/day for first 2 weeks, tapering to 1-2 hours/week by week 6.Suggested activities: guided walkthroughs, shadowing deploys, calendar clean-up, and culture Q&A.Feedback: buddies submit a short 1-page onboarding summary at day 30 and day 60.Risk controls and compliance exceptions:If contractor had access to customer data, require an audit of prior access and explicit re-certification before conversion.Any unresolved IP or contractual obligations must block offer finalization.For', 'provisioning prior to first day as FTE.Ensure at least 3 documented artifacts and 2 knowledge-transfer sessions exist before role transfer.Achieve role competency checkpoints by day 30, 60, 120.Primary stakeholders and responsibilities:Hiring Manager: sponsor the conversion, define role expectations, run technical and cultural assessment, and own 120-day ramp plan.People Ops: lead offer coordination, background checks, compensation calibration, and new-hire admin tasks.IT/Security: validate access changes and complete privileged access approval prior to start date.Finance: assign budget code and start-date for payroll provisioning.Peer Sponsor (Buddy): assigned by hiring manager to provide daily operational support for first 60 days.Pre-offer checklist (Hiring Manager & People Ops):Validate headcount and budget with Finance.Verify contractor status, IP clauses, and any non-compete implications with Legal.Confirm eligibility for full-time benefits and relocation needs.Calibrate'], 'sources': ['dsid_005f7a937cad4b3cbb30d9d93199e22a__embedded-contractor-conversion-playbook-2026.txt']}
{'question_id': 'qst_0110', 'context_precision': 0.0, 'context_recall': 0.0, 'question': 'What is the rollout ramp schedule and time window used for the canary phase when validating adaptive degradation behavior in the Redwood serving runtime?', 'response': '未找到相关文档。', 'gold_answer': 'The canary phase ramps traffic from 1% to 5% to 20% in 30-minute windows, with automated health checks run every 5 minutes.', 'reference': 'The canary phase ramps traffic from 1% to 5% to 20%.\nEach ramp step uses a 30-minute time window.', 'retrieved_contexts': ['Adaptive Degradation Safety & Verification ManualSummary:\\n\\nThis manual defines the safety contracts, verification checks, benchmarking protocols, and operational runbooks we use to validate adaptive degradation and fallback behavior in the Redwood serving runtime. It is intended for runtime engineers, SREs, and release owners who gate runtime and scheduler changes. The document consolidates architecture contracts, test harnesses, profiling playbooks, measurable acceptance criteria, and incident response steps into a single reference.\\n\\nScope:\\n- Covers adaptive degradation (fidelity staircases, model-variant fallback, burst shedding) and scheduler/kernel interactions.\\n- Includes verification checks executed in CI, pre-release staging, and canary phases.\\n- Prescribes profiling and benchmark procedures for SLO verification and performance regressions.\\n- Provides runbooks for on-call and release owners for graceful rollback and mitigation.\\n\\nPrinciples and safety goals:\\n1)', 'outputs.\\n4) Upload artifacts to /artifacts/regressions/.\\n\\nRelated reading and references:\\n- See routing and fallback architecture ADR (link in related_pages).\\n- Profiling playbooks and telemetry contract (links in related_pages).\\n\\nNotes and change log:\\n- 2026-01-15: Initial draft and adoption of verification matrix (S. Rao).\\n- 2026-03-10: Added canary ramp policy and refined kernel warmup contract; added runbook remediation steps (E. Hart, D. Alvarez).\\n\\nIf you are implementing a new scheduler or kernel change, follow this manual start-to-finish: 1) add microbench checks, 2) register threshold baselines, 3) run staging replay, 4) validate VD-* checks, 5) enable canary with the configured ramp, 6) monitor and be ready to rollback.\\n'], 'sources': ['dsid_2e4183d040b341cf843366e2c15402af__adaptive-degradation-safety-verification-manual-2026.txt']}
{'question_id': 'qst_0433', 'context_precision': 0.6488095237933036, 'context_recall': 0.6666666666666666, 'question': "What is Redwood Inference's end-to-end process for rotating secrets and credentials used by production services (including emergency rotation)?", 'response': 'Based on the provided context, Redwood Inference\'s end-to-end process for rotating production secrets and credentials is as follows:\n\n**Preconditions**\n- Identify the secret name/path and all consumers (see Credential Dependencies page).\n- Confirm rotation type: planned vs emergency.\n- Ensure appropriate access (normal approvals or break-glass for emergencies).\n\n**Rotation Patterns**\n- **Pattern A: Dual-valid window (preferred)** — Use when the downstream system can accept two credentials temporarily (e.g., JWT verification keys, API keys with overlap). Steps: generate new credential; store it in Vault under a new version (do not delete old); update consumers to read the latest version and accept both old+new if applicable; deploy changes; observe auth success metrics for at least 30 minutes; disable old credential at the issuer (or mark old as deprecated); remove old credential from Vault after the deprecation window (default 7 days unless...).\n\n**Emergency Rotation During an Incident**\n- **Decision — do we rotate now?** Rotate immediately if any of the following are true: credible evidence of secret exposure; abnormal access patterns consistent with key misuse; vendor reports compromised credentials; attacker has access to systems that can read secrets. If impact is purely availability (no compromise signal), prefer standard mitigation first, as emergency rotation adds risk.\n- **Roles:** Incident Commander (IC) authorizes emergency rotation and prioritizes which secrets first; Ops/SRE executes changes and monitors service health; Security advises on scope and evidence capture; Service owners update consumers and validate functionality.\n- **Procedure:** Declare in incident channel: "Emergency secret rotation in progress" and list candidate secrets (names/paths only); link the break-glass event (if used) and ensure audit logging is on; identify rotation order — Tier 0 first (signing keys, privileged service tokens), Tier 1 next (DB creds, message bus creds, third-party keys with broad scopes); for each secret: confirm consumers from the Credential Dependencies inventory, choose rotation pattern (dual-valid if available; cutover otherwise), rotate issuer-side credential, update Vault/secret storer, restart/redeploy consumers as required, validate per-service health (auth failures, latency, error rate). If failures persist beyond 10 minutes and customer impact is increasing, pause and reassess: rollback vs continue rotation. Record exact timestamps for issuer-side change and consumer rollouts.\n- **Communications:** Do not paste secret values in Slack, tickets, or docs. If customer impact is expected, coordinate with Support for messaging.\n- **After-action requirements:** Ensure break-glass access... (record ends here).\n\n**Post-Rotation Verification Checklist**\nExecute in order:\n1. Confirm consumer rollout completion — verify all services consuming the rotated secret have been restarted/redeployed if required; for services that hot-reload, confirm at least one reload interval has passed; confirm there are no straggler instances on old revisions.\n2. Monitor auth and dependency metrics — watch for 30 minutes minimum (60 minutes for Tier 0): 401/403 rates for service-to-service calls, database authentication failures, third-party provider invalid key errors, sudden increases in retry rate.\n3. Validate critical user journeys — run canary requests against Hosted API chat completion (streaming and non-streaming), embeddings endpoint, console login (if SSO secrets rotated), model registry artifact fetch.\n4. Audit checks — confirm audit logs show... (record ends here).\n\n**Break-glass and Emergency Access Controls (relevant portions)**\n- Break-glass is permitted only when: there is an active customer-impacting incident, or there is a credible suspicion of credential compromise requiring immediate containment, or an operational safety issue requires urgent mitigation and normal approvals are too slow.\n- Break-glass grants time-limited access to Tier 0 / Tier 1 secret read and rotate capabilities, production configuration changes required to restore service, and restricted logs needed for incident diagnosis.\n- Controls: access is time-bound to 60 minutes (extensions require explicit SRE + Security approval); all actions must be logged (audit trails cannot be disabled); any secret retrieved via break-glass must be treated as compromised and rotated if exposure scope is... (record ends here). On-call teams may execute emergency rotations but must follow the break-glass process.', 'gold_answer': 'For planned rotation, the owning service team identifies the secret (Vault path) and **all consumers** via the Credential Dependencies inventory, confirms tier/cadence and access, then rotates using a repeatable pattern: preferably **dual-valid overlap** (generate new credential → write as a new Vault version without deleting old → deploy/update consumers to read latest and, if applicable, accept old+new → monitor auth/health for ≥30 minutes (≥60 for Tier 0) → disable/deprecate the old credential at the issuer → remove the old Vault version after the overlap window, default 7 days) or, if overlap isn\'t possible, a **cutover** (scheduled change window → change issuer-side credential → immediately update Vault → restart/redeploy all consumers → validate/rollback if needed). After any rotation, they complete the verification checklist (ensure all rollouts/reloads completed, monitor auth/dependency metrics, run critical canaries, confirm audit logs and that any break-glass access is revoked) and record the event in a change record.\n\nFor **emergency rotation** (suspected compromise/exposure/anomalies), an incident is opened and "emergency secret rotation" is declared (secret names/paths only), break-glass may be used via the approved workflow for **time-limited (60 min)** Tier 0/1 rotate access with full audit logging; the IC/Security/SRE prioritize and rotate **Tier 0 then Tier 1**, selecting dual-valid where possible or cutover otherwise, updating issuer + Vault and restarting/redeploying all consumers, pausing to reassess if impact worsens, recording exact timestamps, then revoking break-glass immediately and documenting rotated secrets/services plus any follow-up rotations (derived/cached tokens) in the incident timeline and post-incident summary for Security review.', 'reference': 'For planned rotation, the owning service team identifies the secret (Vault path) and all consumers via the Credential Dependencies inventory.\nFor planned rotation, the owning service team confirms tier and cadence and access before rotating.\nPreferred planned rotation method is dual-valid overlap: generate a new credential, write it as a new Vault version without deleting the old, deploy/update consumers to read the latest and if applicable accept both old and new, monitor authentication and service health for at least 30 minutes (at least 60 minutes for Tier 0), then disable or deprecate the old credential at the issuer, and remove the old Vault version after the overlap window (default 7 days).\nIf overlap is not possible, planned rotation uses a cutover: schedule a change window, change the credential at the issuer, immediately update Vault, restart or redeploy all consumers, and validate with rollback if needed.\nAfter any rotation, the team completes a verification checklist that includes ensuring all rollouts/reloads completed, monitoring auth/dependency metrics, running critical canaries, confirming audit logs, and revoking any break-glass access.\nAfter any rotation, the rotation event is recorded in a change record.\nFor emergency rotation (suspected compromise, exposure, or anomalies), an incident is opened and emergency secret rotation is declared using secret names/paths only.\nDuring emergency rotation, break-glass access may be used via the approved workflow for time-limited (60 minute) Tier 0/1 rotate access with full audit logging.\nDuring emergency rotation, IC/Security/SRE prioritize rotating Tier 0 then Tier 1.\nDuring emergency rotation, they use dual-valid overlap where possible or cutover otherwise, update the issuer and Vault, and restart/redeploy all consumers.\nDuring emergency rotation, they pause to reassess if impact worsens and record exact timestamps.\nAfter emergency rotation, break-glass access is revoked immediately and the rotated secrets/services and any follow-up rotations (derived/cached tokens) are documented in the incident timeline and post-incident summary for Security review.', 'retrieved_contexts': ['Production secret rotation runbookPurposeThis runbook provides a repeatable procedure for rotating production secrets used by Redwood services. It covers both planned and emergency rotation patterns.PreconditionsIdentify the secret name/path and all consumers (see Credential Dependencies page).Confirm rotation type: planned vs emergency.Ensure appropriate access (normal approvals or break-glass for emergencies).Rotation patternsPattern A: Dual-valid window (preferred)Use when the downstream system can accept two credentials temporarily (e.g., JWT verification keys, API keys with overlap).Steps:Generate new credential.Store new credential in Vault under a new version (do not delete old).Update consumers to read the latest version and accept both old+new if applicable.Deploy changes.Observe auth success metrics for at least 30 minutes.Disable old credential at the issuer (or mark old as deprecated).Remove old credential from Vault after the deprecation window (default 7 days unless', "Emergency secret rotation during an incidentGoalProvide a coordinated process for rotating secrets quickly during an active incident (suspected compromise or immediate containment need).Decision: do we rotate now?Rotate immediately if any of the following are true:credible evidence of secret exposureabnormal access patterns consistent with key misusevendor reports compromised credentialsattacker has access to systems that can read secretsIf impact is purely availability (no compromise signal), prefer standard mitigation first; emergency rotation adds risk.RolesIncident Commander (IC): authorizes emergency rotation and prioritizes which secrets firstOps/SRE: executes changes and monitors service healthSecurity: advises on scope and evidence captureService owners: update consumers and validate functionalityProcedureDeclare in incident channel: 'Emergency secret rotation in progress' and list candidate secrets (names/paths only).Link the break-glass event (if used) and ensure audit", 'Secret management standard (production)OverviewThis standard defines how Redwood Inference stores, uses, and rotatessecrets and credentialsfor production services. The objective is to reduce the blast radius of credential compromise and ensure rotation is routine and low-risk.DefinitionsSecret: any value that grants access (API keys, database passwords, service tokens, signing keys, OAuth client secrets, private keys).Credential rotation: replacing a secret with a new value and updating all consumers.Emergency rotation: accelerated rotation performed due to suspected compromise or active incident.Approved storage systemsProduction secrets must be stored in one of the following:Redwood Vault (primary)Cloud KMS/HSM-backed secret store where required by compliance (Private deployments may vary)Prohibited:plaintext in Git repositoriesplaintext in Confluence/Google Docslong-lived secrets in CI logshard-coded values in container imagesSecret classificationTier 0: root/admin credentials,', 'Post-rotation verification checklist (production credentials)PurposeThis checklist defines the required verification steps after rotating production secrets to ensure all consumers have updated and service health is stable.Checklist (execute in order)1) Confirm consumer rollout completionVerify all services that consume the rotated secret have been restarted/redeployed if required.For services that hot-reload, confirm at least one reload interval has passed.Confirm there are no straggler instances on old revisions.2) Monitor auth and dependency metricsWatch for 30 minutes minimum (60 minutes for Tier 0):401/403 rates for service-to-service callsdatabase authentication failuresthird-party provider invalid key errorssudden increases in retry rate3) Validate critical user journeysRun canary requests against:Hosted API chat completion (streaming and non-streaming)embeddings endpointconsole login (if SSO secrets rotated)model registry artifact fetch4) Audit checksConfirm audit logs show', 'dual-read / dual-verify where possible (accept old+new for a bounded window)Prefer short-lived tokens derived from an identity provider rather than static shared secretsAvoid single shared credentials across unrelated servicesLogging and auditabilityAccess to Tier 0 and Tier 1 secrets must be auditable (requester identity, time, action).Rotation events must be recorded in a change record (or incident record for emergency).OwnershipSecret owner is the owning service team; Security sets minimum standards and reviews exceptions.On-call teams may execute emergency rotations but must follow the break-glass process.ExceptionsAny exception to cadence or storage must be documented with:rationale and compensating controlsexpiration dateSecurity approvalReferencesSee the Production Secret Rotation Runbook for execution steps.See Break-glass and Emergency Access for emergency access controls.', 'GPU CI credential rotation and image promotion safety kitSummaryPurposeThis document defines a safety-first pattern for: (1) rotating CI credentials used by GPU provisioning and image-promotion pipelines, and (2) promoting GPU runtime images (golden bundles) to production pools with provable checks and fast rollback paths. The kit bundles Terraform/IaC patterns, CI job templates, runtime verification checks, SLO-driven gating, and an operator runbook for emergency credential revocation.Why this mattersCredential rotation without careful orchestration can leave running inference pools unable to pull images, causing degraded capacity and failed deployments.Image promotion (CI -> staging -> prod) must be atomic and verifiable: a mismatched image in a GPU pool can produce model-regression incidents.Past incidents show the majority of image-promotion outages occur during key rotation + cross-account image copy windows. This doc reduces blast radius.ScopeApplies to: all CI pipelines that', "channel: 'Emergency secret rotation in progress' and list candidate secrets (names/paths only).Link the break-glass event (if used) and ensure audit logging is on.Identify rotation order:Tier 0 first (signing keys, privileged service tokens)Tier 1 next (DB creds, message bus creds, third-party keys with broad scopes)For each secret:confirm consumers from the Credential Dependencies inventorychoose rotation pattern (dual-valid if available; cutover otherwise)rotate issuer-side credentialupdate Vault/secret storerestart/redeploy consumers as requiredvalidate per-service health (auth failures, latency, error rate)If failures persist beyond 10 minutes and customer impact is increasing, pause and reassess: rollback vs continue rotation.Record exact timestamps for issuer-side change and consumer rollouts.CommunicationsDo not paste secret values in Slack, tickets, or docs.If customer impact is expected, coordinate with Support for messaging.After-action requirementsEnsure break-glass access", 'Break-glass and emergency access (production)OverviewThis policy governsbreak-glass accessto production systems for emergencies, including emergency secret rotation. Break-glass is intended for rare events and must be auditable.When break-glass is permittedBreak-glass may be used only when:there is an active customer-impacting incident, orthere is a credible suspicion of credential compromise requiring immediate containment, oran operational safety issue requires urgent mitigation and normal approvals are too slowWhat break-glass grantsBreak-glass grants time-limited access to:Tier 0 / Tier 1 secret read and rotate capabilitiesproduction configuration changes required to restore servicerestricted logs needed for incident diagnosisControlsAccess is time-bound to60 minutes(extensions require explicit SRE + Security approval).All actions must be logged (audit trails cannot be disabled).Any secret retrieved via break-glass must be treated as compromised and rotated if exposure scope is'], 'sources': ['dsid_cf06e75be4f94156ac338f62e3ee028b__production-secret-rotation-runbook.txt', 'dsid_3e991a24c7bc4e91b99eb5846b927558__emergency-rotation-during-incident.txt', 'dsid_8c92178c254d467c9854db67aac7c9be__secret-management-standard.txt', 'dsid_74ebb73359364bc791707a92a3227e3a__post-rotation-verification-checklist.txt', 'dsid_f1b208f3637e4987aeab371242a5bea5__gpu-ci-credential-rotation-and-image-promotion-safety.txt', 'dsid_a898d2541b944cdebeec03ec9d9ba130__break-glass-and-emergency-access.txt']}
{'question_id': 'qst_0435', 'context_precision': 0.49999999995, 'context_recall': 1.0, 'question': "How many weekly status updates for Project Orion marked the project as 'At Risk'?", 'response': '3', 'gold_answer': 'Three weekly status updates for Project Orion marked the project as "At Risk" (2026-01-16, 2026-01-23, and 2026-02-06).', 'reference': 'Three weekly status updates for Project Orion were marked as At Risk\nThe At Risk weekly status updates occurred on 2026-01-16, 2026-01-23, and 2026-02-06', 'retrieved_contexts': ['Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-01-16)SummaryOrion is progressing, but we have an emerging integration risk with the runtime router path for fallback decisions.StatusOverall: At RiskRAG: RedProgress this weekPolicy engine skeleton merged behind a feature flag (orion_policy_engine_enabled).Defined fallback precedence rules for model variants (pinning > compatibility > cost).Risks / blockersRuntime router currently applies fallback in a different code path than the control plane expects; we may need a refactor or a shim layer.Timeline risk: if refactor is required, console beta slips by 1–2 weeks.MitigationsSchedule a 90-minute design review with runtime + platform owners.Implement a temporary adapter that maps router decisions into Orion policy evaluation events.Next weekPrototype adapter in staging and validate event schema.OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Alex Martinez (platform), Connor OBrien (runtime routing), Maya Srinivasan (console)', 'Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-01-23)SummaryPrimary risk remains the runtime routing integration. We also uncovered a requirement gap around auditability of fallback decisions for enterprise customers.StatusOverall: At RiskRAG: RedProgress this weekCompleted design review: agreed to keep router decisioning local for latency, but emit standardized decision events.Drafted event schema for policy evaluation and fallback decision logs.Risks / blockersRuntime team concern: additional per-request overhead for emitting decision events.Enterprise requirement: Dedicated customers want an auditable trail of why fallback occurred (capacity vs policy vs compatibility).MitigationsEmit decision events asynchronously and sample at 1% by default; allow customer-tier overrides.Add a "fallback_reason" enum and store only minimal fields required for audit logging.Next weekImplement event emission in runtime router behind a gate; validate overhead in perf canary.OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Connor', 'Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-02-06)SummaryWe hit a console delivery risk: validation and policy preview work is larger than planned, and the beta cohort onboarding may slip.StatusOverall: At RiskRAG: YellowProgress this weekConsole policy editor: added compatibility validation for model variants (client-side).Added basic policy preview panel showing expected canary split and fallback precedence.Risks / blockersPolicy preview requires backend simulation endpoints; current implementation is static and may mislead users.Beta cohort (3 design partners) is waiting on a working preview to sign off.MitigationsScope preview to a read-only backend call for a single route and a single region first.Provide a temporary “preview is approximate” disclaimer until simulation is complete.Next weekAdd control plane simulation endpoint (simulate_policy_v1).Run a beta walkthrough with the solutions engineering team.OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Maya Srinivasan (console), Alex Martinez (control', 'Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-01-09)SummaryProject Orion is the cross-team effort to unify rollout controls (canary, A/B, and automatic fallback) into a single policy surface in the Redwood Console and routing layer.StatusOverall: On TrackRAG: GreenProgress this weekLanded initial API contract for policy evaluation (route selector -> policy engine).Console wireframes approved for the policy editor v1.Defined success criteria for v0 (single-model canary + fallback).Risks / blockersNone material.DecisionsOrion v0 will support canary by percentage and automatic rollback on 5xx rate regression.Next weekImplement policy engine skeleton in platform-control-plane.Start integration with runtime router (read-only evaluation).OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Alex Martinez (platform), Maya Srinivasan (console)'], 'sources': ['dsid_a134a3fd3c244a52a8d3e1ff515fdf58__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-01-16.txt', 'dsid_ce60931a1aea428b8186228ee40bb497__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-01-23.txt', 'dsid_431b0713c42847bbac15f3354776db68__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-02-06.txt', 'dsid_7fc490f8522b486c8f4b665d5189066c__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-01-09.txt']}
